language ESSENCE' 1.0
$ wolf_goat_cabbage: an Essence' model from DCP Rosetta.
$
$ The wolf, goat and cabbage problem is a river crossing puzzle. The story: A farmer with a wolf, a goat, and a cabbage must cross a river by boat. The boat can carry only the farmer and a single item. If left unattended together, the wolf would eat the goat, or the goat would eat the cabbage. How can they cross the river without anything being eaten? The crossing takes the given number of stages. At the first stage everything is on the starting shore, at the last stage everything is on the destination shore, and the boat, with the farmer, changes shore from each stage to the next.
$
$ Save this file as model.eprime. Download `wolf_goat_cabbage.json` and `dcp_convert_essence.py` from the
$ problem's page:
$   https://dcp-bench.github.io/DCP-Rosetta/problems/wolf_goat_cabbage.html
$ Convert the instances once, then run the first one with Savile Row 1.11 (it needs Java); the
$ solution is written to `wolf_goat_cabbage_1.param.solution`:
$   python dcp_convert_essence.py wolf_goat_cabbage.json
$   savilerow model.eprime wolf_goat_cabbage_1.param -run-solver

$ number of stages of the crossing
given stage : int(1..)
letting nst = stage

$ wolf_pos[t], goat_pos[t], cabbage_pos[t], boat_pos[t]: the shore of the wolf, goat, cabbage and boat at stage t
$ (indices 1-based: stage 1 is the data's index 0); true is the destination shore, false the starting shore
find wolf_pos : matrix indexed by [int(1..nst)] of bool
find goat_pos : matrix indexed by [int(1..nst)] of bool
find cabbage_pos : matrix indexed by [int(1..nst)] of bool
find boat_pos : matrix indexed by [int(1..nst)] of bool

such that
$ at the first stage everything is on the starting shore
!wolf_pos[1] /\ !goat_pos[1] /\ !cabbage_pos[1] /\ !boat_pos[1],
$ at the last stage everything is on the destination shore
wolf_pos[nst] /\ goat_pos[nst] /\ cabbage_pos[nst] /\ boat_pos[nst],
$ the boat, with the farmer, changes shore from each stage to the next
forAll t : int(1..nst-1) . boat_pos[t + 1] != boat_pos[t],
$ an item changes shore only when it is on the boat's shore, so the farmer carries it across
forAll t : int(1..nst-1) . (wolf_pos[t + 1] != wolf_pos[t] -> wolf_pos[t] = boat_pos[t]),
forAll t : int(1..nst-1) . (goat_pos[t + 1] != goat_pos[t] -> goat_pos[t] = boat_pos[t]),
forAll t : int(1..nst-1) . (cabbage_pos[t + 1] != cabbage_pos[t] -> cabbage_pos[t] = boat_pos[t]),
$ the boat carries at most one item at a time, so at most one of the wolf, goat and cabbage changes shore
forAll t : int(1..nst-1) . (toInt(wolf_pos[t + 1] != wolf_pos[t]) + toInt(goat_pos[t + 1] != goat_pos[t]) + toInt(cabbage_pos[t + 1] != cabbage_pos[t])) <= 1,
$ the wolf would eat the goat if they are left unattended together: at every stage, they are not on the same shore without the farmer
forAll t : int(1..nst) . !(wolf_pos[t] = goat_pos[t] /\ goat_pos[t] != boat_pos[t]),
$ the goat would eat the cabbage if they are left unattended together: at every stage, they are not on the same shore without the farmer
forAll t : int(1..nst) . !(goat_pos[t] = cabbage_pos[t] /\ goat_pos[t] != boat_pos[t])
